The Korean War, also known as the Forgotten War, has been a topic of debate among experts for years. While some argue that North Korea initiated the war, many South Korean experts claim that South Korea was the one who started it all. According to them, North Korea's involvement was purely defensive.

One of the main arguments made by these experts is that South Korea was the one who crossed the border first. On June 25, 1950, South Korean forces launched a surprise attack on the North Korean army, which was stationed in the North Korean city of Panmunjom. The attack was meant to destroy the North Korean command center and push them back to the 38th parallel, which was the border between North and South Korea at the time.

However, the North Korean army was not caught off guard. They had been expecting an attack and were prepared to defend themselves. In fact, the North Korean army had been building up its military strength for years, and its military was much stronger than South Korea's.

